  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#the-input-element:activation-behaviour\r\n\r\n> The [activation behavior](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#eventtarget-activation-behavior) for [input](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#the-input-element) elements are these steps:\r\n> 1. If this element is not [mutable](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-control-infrastructure.html#concept-fe-mutable) and is not in the [Checkbox](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#checkbox-state-(type=checkbox)) state and is not in the [Radio](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#radio-button-state-(type=radio)) state, then return.\r\n> 2. Run this element's input activation behavior, if any, and do nothing otherwise.\r\n\r\n`disabled` and `readonly` can both affect the `mutable` state, so the step 1 basically checks that if an element is `disabled` or `readonly` (if applicable) and skip the activation behavior.\r\n\r\nBut none of [the `readonly`-applied types](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#do-not-apply) have input activation behavior, so the `readonly` check above is essentially no-op.\r\n\r\nMaybe it can directly check `disabled` in that case, what do you think?",
